     "CsvBody": {
       "description": "The `?export=true` body of the 33 CSV-capable operations (28 `facet`, 4 `signature` and\n`player-position-pivot`): a header row of comma-separated column names, then one row per record,\nCRLF-terminated. Five of the 33 answer with `CsvBodyHeaderless` instead — their report declares no\nheader row — and are not this shape.\n\n**Ask for JSON, not CSV.** `Accept: text/csv` is refused with a `406` before the export is reached,\n`export=true` or not. The export still arrives as `text/csv`, because the response sets that content\ntype itself.",
       "oneOf": [
         {
           "title": "EntitledExport",
           "description": "The export itself: a header row, then one row per record, CRLF-terminated.",
           "type": "string",
           "minLength": 1,
           "pattern": "^[^\\r\\n,]+(,[^\\r\\n,]*)+\\r?\\n"
         },
         {
           "title": "UnentitledExport",
           "description": "An empty body. A caller with view-but-not-access rights on this league gets a `200 text/csv` with a\n`Content-Disposition` and no bytes at all, rather than a `403`. Zero bytes is therefore \"you are not\nentitled to this export\", never \"no rows matched\" — an entitled export with no matching records\nstill carries its header row.",
           "type": "string",
           "maxLength": 0
         }
       ]
     },
     "CsvBodyHeaderless": {
       "description": "The `?export=true` body of the five operations whose report declares no CSV header row. The export\nis one blank CRLF line per record, no header row at all, and the attachment is always named\n`download.csv`.\n\nFour reports, five operat

ions: `offense_summary`, `passing_detail`, `rushing_direction` and\n`receiving_coverage` — the last backs both `facet-receiving-coverage` and\n`facet-defense-coverage-matchup`.\n\nThis is a real `200 text/csv` with a real `Content-Disposition`, which is why those routes stay in\nthe 33: the response is well-formed, it just carries no columns. A client should say so rather than\npretend the column set exists. There is no header line to assert, so `CsvBody`'s pattern would\nreject every one of them — but \"no header\" is not \"anything\": an empty column list can only ever\nproduce nothing at all, or CRLF-terminated blank lines, one per record. `pattern` says exactly that,\nso an upstream HTML error page no longer validates as a successful export. `minLength` is still\nabsent on purpose: a zero-row query returns an empty file, and that is a valid answer.\n\nA zero-byte body is indistinguishable from a headerless export with no records: both are nothing at\nall. Treat an empty response from one of these five as \"no columns and possibly no rows\", and use\nthe JSON form of the operation when the difference matters.",
        "type": "string",
        "pattern": "^(\\r\\n)*$"
      },
